24
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:58,870
With that, let's start with a introductory question for someone who's never worked with ERP.

25
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:03,749
Subjit, how would you explain what it is and why it's central to running a business?

26
00:02:03,759 --> 00:02:05,910
Enterprise resource planning system.

27
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,070
That's the full form of ERP.

28
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:13,510
So we can see like in the full form also enterprise is there.

29
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:19,190
So this is a total software solution for enterprise to run the business.

30
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:30,470
ERP will integrate from finance to procurement to production to inventory to sales to logistics to shipping and each and every business system.

31
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:33,190
So without ERP all these processes will be running in silos.

32
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:41,350
So that will be creating inefficiency that will be creating lot of lagging and there will not be visibility of the business processes at any level.

33
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:52,070
That's the main reason if you see all the Fortune 500 companies are running in one kind of ERPs or some of the companies will be running in multiple ERPs.

34
00:02:52,070 --> 00:03:00,630
I can tell the ERP vendors who are really leaders in ERP industry like SAP, Oracle,

35
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:11,270
Microsoft Dynamics and now from the example I can tell some kind of you know idea like who have never worked with ERP or who doesn't know what is ERP.

36
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:15,110
So let's say without a total software solution like ERP sales department got the sales order.

37
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:24,070
So it will be in an excel and then sales department will be sending those sales order requirement to production department in a mail

38
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:31,750
or excel and then from that production department they will be requesting to the warehouse for having the components for manufacturing the finish groups.

39
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:38,229
So now we can see here there are lot of manual processes there are lot of manual interaction with different departments.

40
00:03:38,229 --> 00:03:52,149
So there is a lot of data lagging and inefficiencies it can happen but with ERP it will never happen because it will be a total same software platform and you can say ERP is the nervous system of an organization to run the business.

41
00:03:52,159 --> 00:04:02,470
Thanks with that background can you walk us through a flow how ERP shows up in day-to-day operations?

42
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:05,110
You mentioned it joins multiple departments together.

43
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:13,429
So maybe could you talk about how a customer order turns into procurement, production, shipping and finally cash.

44
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:15,429
Yeah.

45
00:04:15,429 --> 00:04:20,550
So if we are talking about the basic ERP flows, I would like to mention three basic ERP flows.

46
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:23,749
Order to cash, plan to produce and procure to pay.

47
00:04:23,749 --> 00:04:31,670
Now with order to cache, ERP manages those customer orders like a sales order through delivery to customer invoicing.

48
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:37,270
And then for plan to produce process it will be a demand from sales order.

49
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:41,189
So there will be production order to manufacture the finished goods.

50
00:04:41,189 --> 00:04:51,270
And then with procure to pay process ERP will create purchase order to external vendors for the components which are required to manufacture the product.

51
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:59,749
So I can give one example from electronics manufacturing industry where the products is like laptop,

52
00:04:59,749 --> 00:05:05,430
mobile, cloud computing infrastructure like racks, servers,

53
00:05:05,430 --> 00:05:11,110
then industrial drones and you name any kind of you know electronics component it will be a product for electronics manufacturing industry.

54
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:16,230
So now let's take one example for one familiar product like a laptop.

55
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:18,310
So we all are ordering laptop.

56
00:05:18,310 --> 00:05:29,110
So whenever we are ordering laptop we are basically selecting a different specification right like we are selecting what will be the microprocessor speed what will be the chassis what will be the RAM

57
00:05:29,110 --> 00:05:37,990
so from ERP point of view these are all components and these components are in a bill of material

58
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:46,710
so that's the way we are tracking all the components for manufacturing the laptop now let's say from manufacturing the laptop

59
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:53,510
if sales department got a sales order for manufacturing let's say 100 numbers of laptops.

60
00:05:53,510 --> 00:06:02,790
So for 100 numbers of laptops there will be components all will be needed for procuring if those components will not be in the company stock.

61
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:17,749
So with procurement like a external purchase order to vendors all the components will be coming to company stock then only all these manufacturing orders will be fulfilled with the components to manufacture the 100 numbers of laptops.

62
00:06:17,749 --> 00:06:22,629
So after having the 100 numbers of laptops that will be delivered to customer.

63
00:06:22,629 --> 00:06:28,790
Now if I am just mapping all these processes in these three basic ERP processes.

64
00:06:28,790 --> 00:06:33,749
So order to cash is the sales order and delivery of the 100 numbers of laptops.

65
00:06:33,749 --> 00:06:43,510
Plan to produce will be the manufacturing order which will be internal to the company where this 100 numbers of laptops will be manufactured

66
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:50,710
and then procured to pay cycle will be the external purchase order that went to the vendors for supplying all the components.

67
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:56,550
So in this way you can have all these business processes mapped in the three basic ERP process.

68
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,391
Thank you. That's very helpful.

69
00:06:58,391 --> 00:07:06,390
Now before we dive in further, can you just take a step back and describe how ERP has evolved over time?

70
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:17,189
So in the beginning there were traditional onremise systems, then there was the SAS evolution to SAS and how that set the stage for ML geni and now agent based systems.

71
00:07:17,199 --> 00:07:21,589
Yeah.

72
00:07:21,589 --> 00:07:23,510
So ERP is a old software concept.

73
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:26,469
So it's it's nothing new, right?

74
00:07:26,469 --> 00:07:33,510
So I would say ERP has begun maybe 1990s and then I started working on ERP in onremise software.

75
00:07:33,510 --> 00:07:40,870
So it was around 2005 and then it really evolves from there.

76
00:07:40,870 --> 00:07:51,350
So around 2010 it went to SAS ERP delivery model where all the infrastructures like all the servers application software data and managed by the ERP vendor.

77
00:07:51,350 --> 00:08:01,029
So company doesn't have to take any kind of initiative for installation maintenance of the ERP software and that SAS ERP will be available over the internet to the user.

78
00:08:01,039 --> 00:08:09,749
Now from SAS ERP like I would say 2 3 years back we started with you know AI capability.

79
00:08:09,749 --> 00:08:18,790
So that time we are talking about ML model that is the machine learning algorithms where we can have all the patterns with the big ERP data.

80
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:32,870
From there ML model we'll be having predictive analytics for any kind of business function like anomaly detection like warehouse efficiency improvement and now we are talking about JNI agents.

81
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:37,750
So if we are having a chatbot we are always familiar with the chatbots.

82
00:08:37,750 --> 00:08:45,910
Now if we are thinking about the chatbot that will be directing us how to operate the business process that's

83
00:08:45,910 --> 00:08:57,590
where the ji agents have come like you can just write in a chat GPT like thing and then that ji agent will direct you for the correct insight from the ERP.

84
00:08:57,590 --> 00:08:59,509
So this is the way it really evaluated.

85
00:08:59,519 --> 00:09:07,829
Do you have a real example of what happens when an ERP system goes down or went down?

86
00:09:07,829 --> 00:09:10,230
What broke first in the business and did it escalate?

87
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:12,949
Just walk us through what the chaos might have ensured.

88
00:09:12,959 --> 00:09:23,110
Yeah, it will be a complete chaos obviously because as I said earlier ERP system is the nervous system of a business.

89
00:09:23,110 --> 00:09:25,509
So if ERP system goes down then production order will be halted.

90
00:09:25,519 --> 00:09:32,150
Procurement will not be happening because buyer will not be sending the purchase order to vendors.

91
00:09:32,150 --> 00:09:34,070
Shipment will be delayed.

92
00:09:34,070 --> 00:09:35,590
So order will not be fulfilled.

93
00:09:35,590 --> 00:09:38,949
So there will be lot of revenue loss.

94
00:09:38,949 --> 00:09:42,870
So to have this kind of downtime because ERP is a software right.

95
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:45,350
So for any kind of software this update maintenance is required.

96
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:51,110
So to avoid this kind of disruption there are planned downtime that is required always

97
00:09:51,120 --> 00:10:02,230
for having the update in the ERP for latest kind of uh software update and that time there will be different strategy

98
00:10:02,230 --> 00:10:11,030
that organization needs to take like there will be a lot of you know offline entries like the manual entries that will be done and after that it will be entered in the ERP.

99
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:13,750
So in that way those kind of downtime can be managed.

100
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:15,910
Got it?

101
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:21,110
Now that we've covered the basics of ERP, let's talk about implementation.

102
00:10:21,110 --> 00:10:26,791
What does an ERP implementation actually look like from start to go live? Yeah.

103
00:10:26,791 --> 00:10:30,230
So ERP implementation is a structured process.

104
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:34,949
I would say it will be six to seven stages.

105
00:10:34,949 --> 00:10:42,949
So it will be starting with the project preparation where the business problems will be finalized and the project will be finalized.

106
00:10:42,949 --> 00:10:50,790
Project manager and project team will be assigned and then there will be business blueprint phase where that business problem will be discussed

107
00:10:50,790 --> 00:10:58,230
with business team to understand what will be the changes in the ERP system and then as is and 2B documents will be prepared and

108
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:09,910
then in the next phase it will be system design and realization phase where project team will be changing in the ERP system.

109
00:11:09,910 --> 00:11:24,389
So it can be a enhancement for the existing functionality or it can be a new transaction that is needed for users or it can be the integration with the third party systems like it can be MES system

110
00:11:24,389 --> 00:11:39,269
that needs to be integrated with the ERP that is the manufacturing execution system which is very common in electronics manufacturing industry because all the production operations in electronics manufacturing needs to be tracked at very granular level.

111
00:11:39,269 --> 00:11:41,509
So, MEES system will be integrated.

112
00:11:41,509 --> 00:11:56,470
For that, we need to develop different framework and then we have to also do different framework for businessto business communication with the different customers and vendors like we have to send maybe inventory data to the customers

113
00:11:56,470 --> 00:12:05,190
and they want to have the visibility of company stock how the raw materials are coming according to that they will be placing the orders.

114
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:19,350
So having said this will be the phase where ERP team will be really doing the changes in the system and then there will be the next stage where all these changes will be tested.

115
00:12:19,350 --> 00:12:21,750
It can be testing within ERP system.

116
00:12:21,750 --> 00:12:24,790
It can be testing with the MES system.

117
00:12:24,790 --> 00:12:26,710
For MES system there will be different team.

118
00:12:26,710 --> 00:12:39,350
It can be the testing with the vendor or customer team because vendor and customer also needs to test their own functionality with this B2B communication.

119
00:12:39,350 --> 00:12:50,069
So after all this testing successful there will be final preparation and go live phase where there will be user training because user needs to know what will be the new functionalities.

120
00:12:50,069 --> 00:13:02,069
Already some of the users like super users they will be testing those functionalities and after their testing certification only all these new changes will be going to the production.

121
00:13:02,069 --> 00:13:15,030
So for all other users there should be training to operate the new functionalities and then for new site implementation there will be data migration like there may be some of the new inventory

122
00:13:15,030 --> 00:13:21,190
that needs to be updated in the new site and there will be different new beans that needs to be created.

123
00:13:21,190 --> 00:13:23,750
So those are some kind of example for the data migration.

124
00:13:23,750 --> 00:13:31,990
So after all this preparation training data migration strategy program then there will be go live.

125
00:13:31,990 --> 00:13:36,230
So in this phase it can be technical go live and after that it can be business gloive.

126
00:13:36,230 --> 00:13:41,509
Sometime what will happen the bigger changes which can affect the whole ERP system globally.

127
00:13:41,519 --> 00:13:47,670
Those changes will be sent at the system downtime.

128
00:13:47,670 --> 00:13:49,350
So the business go live will not be happening immediately.

129
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:53,910
that business go live will be happening after the technical go live.

130
00:13:53,910 --> 00:14:06,069
So after go live obviously we need to support the system if there will be any kind of issues usually it will be saying as hyperare support like we have to resolve that issues very fast.

131
00:14:06,069 --> 00:14:09,590
So there will be very strict SLA for that.

132
00:14:09,590 --> 00:14:16,870
So after this hyperare support it will be handed over to support team and that will be the end of that project.

133
00:14:16,870 --> 00:14:20,150
So this is a pretty good flow of the ERP implementation methodology.

134
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:22,470
So you did call out integrations.

135
00:14:22,470 --> 00:14:24,310
You give an example of that.

136
00:14:24,310 --> 00:14:29,990
You talked about data migration and then you also said in some cases there are enhancements needed.

137
00:14:29,990 --> 00:14:31,829
Do you have an example of an enhancement?

138
00:14:31,839 --> 00:14:35,110
Yeah, sure.

139
00:14:35,110 --> 00:14:47,910
So let's say we need to have one interface that needs to build so that we can send the inventory data to a customer and we do not have this B2B communication with that customer.

140
00:14:47,910 --> 00:14:52,069
So we have to do the changes in ERP system and

141
00:14:52,079 --> 00:15:03,189
we have to do the testing with the file whatever we are sending whether that file is getting received by the customer or not.

142
00:15:03,189 --> 00:15:10,470
So this is a kind of enhancement that we have to do in our system as well as we have to do integration testing between customer system.

143
00:15:10,470 --> 00:15:14,310
So this is an example like if we are building some kind of you know new interface.

144
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:21,670
Could you maybe share an example of an implementation that went well and talk about what made it succeed.

145
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:31,350
So I would like to share one of the biggest implementation that I was a part of and that went very well.

146
00:15:31,350 --> 00:15:37,910
So it was SAP S4ANA implementation 3 to four years back.

147
00:15:37,910 --> 00:15:44,150
So as I said SAP is one of the biggest ERP vendor and their latest technology is for HANA.

148
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:47,590
So HANA is inmemory platform.

149
00:15:47,590 --> 00:15:50,790
So the transactions of the ERP will be very fast.

150
00:15:50,790 --> 00:15:56,870
So that's the main benefit by going to SAP S4 HANA.

151
00:15:56,870 --> 00:15:58,949
So earlier we are having onremise system as I said earlier.

152
00:15:58,949 --> 00:16:01,990
So it's a kind of evaluation.

153
00:16:01,990 --> 00:16:06,150
So we went to cloud and to SAP S4 HANA cloud.

154
00:16:06,150 --> 00:16:17,189
So that time 3 to 4 years ago there is no program available from SAP like today it is available SAP rise where you can go to private cloud that time it was not there.

155
00:16:17,189 --> 00:16:28,069
So we have to basically go through our own methodology own framework own testing strategy own role strategy role means like the authorization what authorization we'll be having for the user.

156
00:16:28,069 --> 00:16:31,189
So like warehouse manager will be having different authorization.

157
00:16:31,189 --> 00:16:43,350
Warehouse operator who is only doing putter or picking there will be different authorization and then all the transaction are kind of a changing with the new SAP espana system.

158
00:16:43,350 --> 00:16:51,590
So we have to go for a rigorous testing and a rigorous training because there are a lot of customization in our system.

159
00:16:51,590 --> 00:16:59,430
So to adopt all this new process, we really need to test each and every process so that nothing will fail in the production.

160
00:16:59,430 --> 00:17:14,069
And we went for the big bang go live where all the 100 plus sites are affected worldwide like there are sites in US, Mexico, Brazil, in Asia and in Europe.

161
00:17:14,079 --> 00:17:21,189
So you can understand there are multilingual users in different time zone in different business process.

162
00:17:21,189 --> 00:17:27,990
So it was really not easy to go with direct implementation.

163
00:17:27,990 --> 00:17:30,390
So we went with a little bit phased out implementation.

164
00:17:30,390 --> 00:17:36,230
But the final implementation of SAP forhana it was a kind of a big bang.

165
00:17:36,230 --> 00:17:46,150
So with this implementation there is a good amount of savings like $1.8 $8 million yearly cost savings

166
00:17:46,150 --> 00:17:52,390
and there is a lot of implementation experiences that has been shared in different SAP conferences.

167
00:17:52,390 --> 00:17:56,549
So I would say it was a big strategic implementation that I would like to share.

168
00:17:56,559 --> 00:18:01,350
What was it specifically during the implementation that caused the success?

169
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:08,390
I would say it's a proper planning, team collaboration and team coordination because there are multiple teams.

170
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:16,310
There is a business team, there is a technical team, there is partner teams, there is a different vendor team.

171
00:18:16,310 --> 00:18:24,870
So it's a proper planning that is key of success and there is a lot of webinars that we have done.

172
00:18:24,870 --> 00:18:33,669
We have gone for the different languages also for doing the user training and obviously there is a technical upgradation.

173
00:18:33,669 --> 00:18:38,310
So that has been heavily supported by ERP vendor SAP.

174
00:18:38,310 --> 00:18:46,470
So all these things all click together and that's the main success we got with very little downtime and disrupting the business.

175
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:50,549
So proper planning was a key to the success.

176
00:18:50,549 --> 00:18:58,230
Now could you share an example of an implementation that went sideways and why and what were the early warning signs?

177
00:18:58,230 --> 00:19:09,430
Yeah, I can give a recent example from our first SAP BTP project in inventory management where pre-receiving process was defined and implemented in more than 40 sites globally.

178
00:19:09,430 --> 00:19:12,390
But initially it was a failure.

179
00:19:12,390 --> 00:19:15,111
So what was the reason? So what is SAP BP?

180
00:19:15,111 --> 00:19:19,830
Let me tell first.

181
00:19:19,830 --> 00:19:33,430
So SAP BP is the latest innovative platform from SAP where you can develop your own solution that will not be affecting ERP and all the solution will be connecting to ERP with different APIs.

182
00:19:33,430 --> 00:19:35,830
So that is the advantage.

183
00:19:35,830 --> 00:19:39,510
So you are not doing lot of customization to the ERP system.

184
00:19:39,510 --> 00:19:47,830
So now in this case we have developed this pre receiving platform where user will not be able

185
00:19:47,830 --> 00:19:55,990
to enter the material whatever is coming from the vendor because those materials will not be having the correct number

186
00:19:55,990 --> 00:20:04,310
or maybe the purchase order is not having the capacity for doing the good receipt or maybe some wrong descriptions or some wrong values are there.

187
00:20:04,310 --> 00:20:09,111
So whatever is the reason user is not able to do the good receipt in the ERP. So that's the business problem.

188
00:20:09,111 --> 00:20:14,150
So after developing this platform what will happen?

189
00:20:14,150 --> 00:20:20,549
Buyer can go into this platform and see what was wrong with that particular good receipt and then buyer can correct that.

190
00:20:20,549 --> 00:20:26,150
So with this functionalities we went live with all the processes and testing everything.

191
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:35,830
But after going live user came back and told that they are not able to do the physical inventory process.

192
00:20:35,830 --> 00:20:37,430
So what is that physical inventory process?

193
00:20:37,430 --> 00:20:47,669
like you will be going to the warehouse physically and you will be counting that and that is needed for their daily checking of the inventory.

194
00:20:47,669 --> 00:20:49,510
So then again we have to stop.

195
00:20:49,510 --> 00:20:55,029
So from project management point of view it was a strategic project so it was a very big failure there was escalation.

196
00:20:55,029 --> 00:20:57,110
So we have to again start from the beginning.

197
00:20:57,110 --> 00:21:11,990
So there will be more budget obviously needed because the project team will work with the new functionalities and then after doing all these functionalities implemented like the physical inventory and then the other functionalities were there they will be required different print out

198
00:21:11,990 --> 00:21:19,750
and different reports then it went live and now it is in 40 sites it is running successfully.

